-- Cat Act 2011 (WA), Act No. 055 of 2011, consolidated version 00-l0-01 -- shared ontology.
--
-- This module carries the types the operative modules share. It states no rule:
-- every `DECLARE` here is a fact-shape, and every predicate over it lives in the
-- module for the Division that enacts it.
--
-- Source: sources/cat-act-2011-00-l0-01.txt, converted from the register's own
-- mrdoc_48898.docx (sha256 5321f420afaeaf66a4dd7e137a6d32993e277dcb79d532c6c362
-- c2b97b60fb50). Section references are to that extract; line numbers cited in
-- comments are its line numbers.
--
-- NO IMPORTS. The `l4` on this machine ships without its standard library
-- (incident H-04 of the retail-barring-orders-bill-2025 register) and an
-- unresolvable IMPORT typechecks clean (H-03), so an `IMPORT prelude` line here
-- would be a claim this file cannot cash.
--
-- HOUSE STYLE. Inert style: the statutory words ride as inert strings in the
-- scaffolding, and the operative atoms are short names the ladder can print.
-- Field names start at the complement, because the Saxon genitive already
-- supplies "is" and "has" -- `cat's `sterilised`` reads "the cat IS sterilised".
--
-- SCOPE DISCIPLINE. Everything the Act leaves to be `prescribed` (s 3(1):
-- "prescribed means prescribed under regulations made under this Act") is a
-- fact supplied from outside this encoding. The Cat Regulations 2012 and the
-- Cat (Uniform Local Provisions) Regulations 2013 were not retrieved, so no
-- provision here can make such a fact true; that is a real gap, recorded, not a
-- defect of the discipline.

§ `Part 1 -- Preliminary`

§§ `3 -- Terms used`

-- s 3(1): `cat` means an animal of the species felis catus or a hybrid of that
-- species. Carried as an identity: nothing in the encoded slice turns on the
-- species question, only on whether two references are to the same cat.
--
-- `age in months` rather than a date of birth: every age-sensitive provision in
-- the encoded Divisions (ss 5(1), 9(2)(a), 14(1), 14(3), 18(1), 18(3)) asks
-- whether the cat "has reached 6 months of age", which is a question about the
-- cat at the moment the duty is tested, not a date calculation. Recording a
-- birth date and an assessment date would invite a `daydate` computation this
-- toolchain cannot do (H-04) and the Act does not ask for.
DECLARE Cat HAS
    `name`                           IS A STRING
    `age in months`                  IS A NUMBER
    -- s 3(1): `microchipped` means implanted with a microchip in a prescribed
    -- manner. The prescribed manner is a fact supplied: see SCOPE DISCIPLINE.
    `microchipped`                   IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 3(1): `sterilised` means made permanently infertile by a surgical
    -- procedure. s 18(1) additionally requires that it be done BY A
    -- VETERINARIAN, which is a fact about the procedure and not about the cat,
    -- so it is a field of its own rather than folded in here.
    `sterilised`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `sterilised by a veterinarian`   IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 3(1): `registered` means registered under section 9.
    `registered`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 6(1): the duty is that the cat is wearing ITS registration tag, which
    -- s 3(1) defines as the tag given to the owner under s 11(1)(c) -- so a
    -- different cat's tag does not discharge it.
    `wearing its registration tag`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `in a public place`              IS A BOOLEAN

-- s 3(1): `authorised person`, three limbs, kept as an enum because the Act
-- never asks whether someone answers to more than one of them.
DECLARE AuthorisedPerson IS ONE OF
    `a police officer`                                               -- s 3(1)(a)
    `a person designated under the Public Health Act 2016 s 24(1)`    -- s 3(1)(ba)
    `a person appointed under the Local Government Act 1995 s 9.10(2)` -- s 3(1)(b)

-- s 9(2)(a) refuses an application by "a child under 18 years of age", and
-- s 4(1)(c) makes a child's parent or guardian the owner instead. Both turn on
-- the same partition, so it is one type with two alternatives.
DECLARE Age IS ONE OF
    `an adult`
    `a child under 18 years of age`

DECLARE Person HAS
    `name`   IS A STRING
    `age`    IS AN Age
    -- s 3(1): `approved cat breeder` means a person who has an approval to
    -- breed cats under s 37. Carried as a fact because Subdivision 4 of Part 3
    -- Division 4 (ss 35-40), which grants the approval, is outside the encoded
    -- slice -- so nothing here can make it true.
    `an approved cat breeder`   IS A BOOLEAN

-- s 3(1): `veterinarian` has the meaning given in the Veterinary Practice Act
-- 2021 s 3. An external instrument, not retrieved: whether a given person is a
-- veterinarian is a fact supplied.
DECLARE LocalGovernment HAS
    `name`   IS A STRING

§§ `4 -- Term used: owner`

-- s 4(1): `owner` means (a) for a registered cat, the registered owner; or
-- (b) for an unregistered cat, the person who ordinarily keeps and cares for
-- it; or (c) where that person is a child under 18, that child's parent or
-- guardian.
--
-- Three limbs of ONE definition, and they are ordered by the cat's registration
-- status, so the shape is a record of the facts each limb reads rather than an
-- enum of outcomes. `who the registered owner is` is MAYBE by design: for an
-- unregistered cat there is none, and the absence is what routes s 4(1) to
-- limb (b).
DECLARE Ownership HAS
    `the registered owner`                      IS A MAYBE Person
    `who ordinarily keeps and cares for the cat` IS A MAYBE Person
    -- s 4(2): for a cat that is not registered but IS microchipped, the person
    -- recorded as owner in a microchip database is TAKEN, in the absence of
    -- evidence to the contrary, to be the person who ordinarily keeps and cares
    -- for the cat. A deeming provision with a rebuttal, so both halves are
    -- facts: who the database records, and whether there is contrary evidence.
    `recorded as owner in a microchip database`  IS A MAYBE Person
    `there is evidence to the contrary`          IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 4(1)(c): only reached where the limb (b) person is a child.
    `the parent or guardian of that child`       IS A MAYBE Person

§ `Part 2 -- Registration, identification and sterilisation of cats`

§§ `Certificates a veterinarian gives`

-- ss 14(2) and 18(2)(a) each turn on a certificate given by a veterinarian
-- stating that the procedure "may adversely affect the health and welfare of
-- the cat". Two different certificates for two different procedures, and
-- ss 14(3) and 18(3) each say such a certificate "cannot apply in respect of a
-- cat that is under 6 months of age".
--
-- `the age in months of the cat when the certificate was given` is recorded
-- because that is what ss 14(3) and 18(3) test, and because the interaction
-- between those subsections and the duties in ss 14(1) and 18(1) is the subject
-- of an incident: the duty attaches at 6 months, and the certificate that would
-- excuse it cannot exist before 6 months.
DECLARE VetCertificate HAS
    `about`                          IS A Procedure
    `given by a veterinarian`        IS A BOOLEAN
    `the age in months of the cat when it was given` IS A NUMBER
    `it applies in respect of the cat` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE Procedure IS ONE OF
    `implantation of a microchip`    -- s 14(2)
    `sterilisation`                  -- s 18(2)(a)

§§ `The facts a duty under Part 2 is tested against`

-- One record per (cat, owner, local government) situation, because every duty
-- in Divisions 1 to 3 is a duty of "the owner of a cat" and every exception
-- reads facts about the owner, the cat, or the district.
DECLARE Situation HAS
    `cat`                        IS A Cat
    `owner`                      IS A Person
    `local government of the district where the cat is ordinarily kept` IS A LocalGovernment
    `the cat is registered with that local government` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 5(2)(a): "the cat has been kept by the person for less than 14 days".
    `days the cat has been kept by the owner` IS A NUMBER
    -- s 5(2)(b): "the person has been resident in the State for less than 14
    -- days".
    `days the owner has been resident in the State` IS A NUMBER
    -- The `prescribed` exemptions: s 5(2)(c), s 6(2), s 18(2)(c). Facts
    -- supplied, per SCOPE DISCIPLINE -- the regulations were not retrieved.
    `of a class prescribed as exempt from registration` IS A BOOLEAN
    `of a class prescribed as exempt from wearing a tag in a public place` IS A BOOLEAN
    `of a class prescribed as exempt from sterilisation` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- ss 14(2), 18(2)(a): at most one certificate of each kind is relevant, and
    -- either may be absent.
    `certificate about microchipping`   IS A MAYBE VetCertificate
    `certificate about sterilisation`   IS A MAYBE VetCertificate
    -- s 18(2)(b): the cat is owned, FOR THE PURPOSE OF BREEDING, by an approved
    -- cat breeder. Two conditions, and the purpose is not a fact about the
    -- breeder, so it is recorded here and not on `Person`.
    `owned for the purpose of breeding`  IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Interpretation Act 1984 (WA) s 63: "Where no time is fixed or allowed
    -- within which an act or thing shall be done, such act or thing shall be
    -- done with all convenient speed and as often as occasion arises."
    --
    -- ss 5(1), 14(1) and 18(1) fix no time. So none of them is breached by the
    -- mere arrival of the cat's sixth month: the owner must register, microchip
    -- and sterilise with all convenient speed thereafter, and is in breach only
    -- if they do not. This fact is what s 63 supplies, and it is an input rather
    -- than a rule because whether a course of conduct was speedy enough is a
    -- question of degree that no encoding decides. FORK-2 in the fork register
    -- records the reading and the one rejected.
    `the owner has proceeded with all convenient speed` IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `9 -- Registration: the facts an application is decided on`

-- s 8(1)-(2) and s 9(1)-(7). The application is a record of what s 9(2)'s
-- closed list and s 9(5)-(6) read.
DECLARE Application HAS
    `applicant`      IS A Person
    `situation`      IS A Situation
    `for`            IS AN ApplicationKind
    -- s 9(2)(e): the applicant has been convicted within the previous 3 years
    -- of 2 or more offences against this Act, the Dog Act 1976 or the Animal
    -- Welfare Act 2002. The Act counts offences across three instruments, so
    -- the count is the fact, not the convictions themselves.
    `convictions in the previous 3 years for offences against this Act, the Dog Act 1976 or the Animal Welfare Act 2002` IS A NUMBER
    -- s 9(5): the local government MAY require documents or information within
    -- a specified time of not more than 21 days; s 9(6): it may refuse to
    -- consider the application if the applicant does not comply in time.
    `a requirement under section 9(5) was made`      IS A BOOLEAN
    `the specified time in days`                     IS A MAYBE NUMBER
    `the applicant complied within the specified time` IS A BOOLEAN

-- s 8(1): an owner may apply for the GRANT or the RENEWAL of registration, and
-- s 9(1)-(2) treats the two alike throughout. Kept as a type anyway, because
-- s 9(1) states two different decisions and a reader checking the encoding
-- against the section should see both.
DECLARE ApplicationKind IS ONE OF
    `the grant of registration`
    `the renewal of registration`

-- s 9(1): the decision the local government makes. s 9(2) makes refusal
-- mandatory in the listed cases and, by "if, and only if", forbids it
-- otherwise; s 9(6) adds a refusal to CONSIDER, which is not one of the
-- outcomes s 9(1) offers. That gap is the subject of an incident.
DECLARE Decision IS ONE OF
    `grant or renew the registration`
    `refuse the application`
    `refuse to consider the application`
